{
  "requirement": "TBV owns the model conversation, supported backend integrations, tool\ndispatch, and structured submission used by its ask builder.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The subject is a broad ownership statement. It says TBV owns four things: the model conversation, the supported backend integrations, tool dispatch, and structured submission. Finer rules for submission, isolation and provider failure sit in their own requirements: `submission.*`, `tool_agent.isolation`, `tool_agent.provider_failure`, `fixed_tools` and others. Those are not reassessed here.\n\nI mapped each of the four areas to tests and found no gap.\n\n**Model conversation**\n- `agent/tests.rs#fn[application_tool_factories_create_fresh_state_for_each_agent_call]` and `fn[application_tools_preserve_graph_tools_in_the_same_conversation]` check the recorded requests. They assert the tool list on every turn, and tool results delivered in order as the last message of the next turn.\n- `fn[behavioral_adequacy_uses_domain_contents_and_returns_a_typed_fingerprinted_answer]` asserts the exact system prompt and the order of the user message contents. It also checks the typed `submit_result` schema.\n- `fn[concurrent_tool_agents_isolate_histories_pages_and_submissions_and_share_reads]` and `fn[agent_executions_isolate_conversations_and_dependency_traces]` check that concurrent conversations stay separate. They assert per-conversation call counts and results.\n\n**Supported backend integrations**\n\nThe supported backends are Bedrock, OpenAI and Ollama, per `AgentConfig` in `config.rs`.\n- OpenAI: `configured.rs#mod[tests]/fn[supplied_openai_backend_runs_independent_calls_against_a_local_transport]` runs a real exchange against a local server. It checks model, output-token limit, tool names and order, the `submit_result` schema, and the prompt content. It also checks that tool results go back on the second turn, that runs are independent, and that no secrets are persisted.\n- Ollama: `fn[supplied_ollama_backend_delivers_tools_and_results_over_native_chat]` also runs a real local exchange. It checks the `/api/chat` path, the bearer credential, options, the tool schema, and the tool result in a `tool` message.\n- Bedrock has no end-to-end local transport test. That would need AWS services. The Bedrock-specific logic is covered by three tests in `providers.rs#mod[tests]`, each marked for the subject:\n  - `fn[typed_client_calls_preserve_order_and_native_history]` checks the normalization of client tool calls and that history, order, raw response and origin are preserved. It also checks that normalizing twice changes nothing.\n  - `fn[bedrock_native_option_types_are_checked_and_only_max_tokens_is_defaulted]` checks option validation and defaulting.\n  - `fn[cache_checkpoints_are_requested_for_caching_models]` checks which models get cache checkpoints.\n\n**Tool dispatch**\n- `fn[application_tools_preserve_graph_tools_in_the_same_conversation]` dispatches graph tools and an application tool together. It asserts the delivered results, paging, and recorded dependencies.\n- `fn[source_tools_keep_search_discovery_separate_from_delivered_source_reads]` dispatches search and source-read tools and asserts the observed dependency.\n\n**Structured submission**\n- Every scripted conversation ends in `submit_result`, and the typed answers are asserted: `true`, the string results, and the structured `TestReview`.\n- The submit schema is asserted in the OpenAI, Ollama and behavioral-adequacy tests.\n\nI saw no requirement-violating behavior that these tests would accept. The remaining detail is the responsibility of the refining requirements.",
